    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "description",
      "value": "Redesign the experiment to explicitly characterize solution space geometry rather than just fitting capacity. Define analytic constraint functions (e.g., polynomial systems with controlled degree and domain), specify network architecture (2-layer MLP, ReLU, He initialization), implement multi-start optimization (50+ random initializations per constraint set) to sample solution space connectivity, measure solution space rigidity via: (1) volume of solution set (via Monte Carlo sampling near solutions), (2) curvature analysis (Hessian spectrum at critical points), (3) basin connectivity (distance between solutions from different starts), and (4) critical point distribution. Train with AdamW with learning rate scheduling, track convergence to multiple solutions, and determine if minimal N_c remains constant across w by observing whether new solutions emerge or existing ones persist in connected components."
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "skill",
      "value": "None (custom ML experiment pipeline)"
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "expected_prediction",
      "value": "Solution space rigidity will manifest as constant N_c(w) for small networks with connected solution basins, while larger networks will show growing N_c(w) with fragmented solution spaces. Curvature analysis will reveal flatter landscapes for rigid regimes, and multi-start sampling will show high basin overlap at low N_c that decreases with network width."
